.TH PATCH 2
.SH NAME
parsepatch, freepatch, revpatch, splittext, findhunk, placehunks, applydiff \- read and apply unified diffs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ta \w'Patch*\ \ \ 'u
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <patch.h>
.PP
.ft L
.nf
.ta \w'typedef 'u +\w'struct 'u +\w'Ptext\ \ 'u
typedef struct Pline {
	char	*s;
	int	n;
} Pline;
.PP
.ft L
.nf
typedef struct Ptext {
	Pline	*l;
	int	n;
} Ptext;
.PP
.ft L
.nf
typedef struct Phunk {
	int	oldln;
	int	oldcnt;
	int	newln;
	int	newcnt;
	int	lead;
	int	trail;
	Ptext	old;
	Ptext	new;
	int	lnum;
} Phunk;
.PP
.ft L
.nf
typedef struct Pdiff {
	char	*old;
	char	*new;
	int	oldmode;
	int	newmode;
	int	flags;
	Phunk	*h;
	int	nh;
	int	lnum;
} Pdiff;
.PP
.ft L
.nf
typedef struct Patch {
	Pdiff	*d;
	int	nd;
	char	*buf;
} Patch;
.fi
.PP
.ta \w'Patch*\ \ \ 'u
.B
Patch*	parsepatch(char *s, long n, int p)
.PP
.B
void	freepatch(Patch *pt)
.PP
.B
void	revpatch(Patch *pt)
.PP
.B
void	splittext(Ptext *t, char *s, long n)
.PP
.B
int	findhunk(Pdiff *d, Phunk *h, Ptext *t, int lo, int drift)
.PP
.B
int	placehunks(Pdiff *d, Ptext *t, int *at)
.PP
.B
char*	applydiff(Pdiff *d, Ptext *t, long *np)
.PP
.B
void*	emalloc(ulong n)
.PP
.B
void*	erealloc(void *p, ulong n)
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines read unified diffs, with
.IR git 's
headers, and apply them to text in memory, as
.IR patch (1)
does to files.
.PP
.I Parsepatch
reads the
.I n
bytes at
.I s
and returns a
.B Patch
that holds a
.B Pdiff
for each file, in the order of the patch, and a copy of the bytes.
It skips the text around the diffs.
.I P
is the number of slashes to strip from each name, as
.I patch
.B -p
does, or \-1 for 1 when a diff has
.IR git 's
header and 0 when it does not.
A
.BR Pdiff 's
.B old
and
.B new
are the names, stripped and cleaned, or nil for
.BR /dev/null .
A name that is not local, as
.IR islocal (2)
has it, makes the patch fail.
.B Oldmode
and
.B newmode
are
.IR git 's
modes, or 0.
.B Flags
holds
.B Pgit
for a diff with
.IR git 's
header,
.B Pnew
for one that makes its file,
.B Pdel
for one that removes it,
.B Pren
and
.B Pcopy
for a rename and a copy, and
.B Pzero
when the caller takes the diff as made with no context, as
.I patch
.B -z
does;
.I parsepatch
never sets it.
.B Lnum
is the line of the patch where the diff begins.
.I Parsepatch
returns nil, with the error string set, for a patch that it cannot read and for a binary patch.
.I Freepatch
frees a
.BR Patch .
.I Revpatch
turns each diff around, so that it changes the new file into the old.
.PP
A
.B Phunk
has the numbers of its header,
.BR oldln ,
.BR oldcnt ,
.B newln
and
.BR newcnt ;
the number of context lines before its first change,
.BR lead ,
and after its last,
.BR trail ;
its old lines, context and removed, in
.BR old ,
and its new lines, context and added, in
.BR new ;
and the line of the patch where its header is,
.BR lnum .
A
.B Ptext
is an array of lines, and each
.B Pline
points into the bytes it came from:
.B n
counts the bytes of the line with its newline, which the last line of a file may lack.
.I Splittext
fills a
.B Ptext
with the lines of the
.I n
bytes at
.IR s ,
in an array that the caller frees.
.PP
.I Findhunk
finds where the hunk
.I h
of the diff
.I d
applies in
.IR t ,
at a line no earlier than
.IR lo ,
and returns that line, counted from 0, or \-1.
The hunk applies where its old lines are exactly.
.I Findhunk
looks first at the line the hunk names plus
.IR drift ,
and then one line further each way in turn, below before above, as
.IR git 's
apply does.
A hunk that names the first line applies there or nowhere,
and one with no context after its changes must end at the end of
.IR t ,
unless the diff is
.BR Pzero .
.PP
.I Placehunks
finds each hunk of
.I d
in
.I t
in turn, each after the one before and moved by as much, and puts its line in
.IR at ,
which has room for
.B d->nh
numbers.
It returns 0, or \-1 with the error string set when a hunk does not apply.
.I Applydiff
places the hunks of
.I d
in
.IR t ,
and returns the text they make, in an array that the caller frees, and its length in
.BR *np .
It returns nil, with the error string set, when a hunk does not apply.
.PP
A program applying hunks one at a time, as an editor applies them to the text of a window,
can call
.I findhunk
with
.I lo
the line after the last hunk it applied and
.I drift
how far that hunk moved.
.PP
The library allocates with two routines that the program provides, as
.IR html (2)
does:
.I emalloc
returns zeroed memory and
.I erealloc
resizes as
.I realloc
does, and neither returns when it fails.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libpatch
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR diff (1),
.IR patch (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Parsepatch
sets the error string to the line of the patch and what is wrong with it:
a hunk header it cannot read, a line in a hunk that is not context, a removal or an addition,
or more of them than the header counts, a patch that ends inside a hunk, a name it cannot
strip or that is not local, a quoted name with no closing quote or with an escape that
.IR git 's
quoting does not make
(it makes
.BR \a ,
.BR \b ,
.BR \f ,
.BR \n ,
.BR \r ,
.BR \t ,
.BR \v ,
.BR \\ ,
.B \"
and three octal digits, the first 0 to 3, and never a NUL),
a rename or copy with one name, and a binary patch.
.I Applydiff
also fails when a hunk would leave a line without a newline inside the file:
a hunk whose last added line has none, with text or another hunk's lines after it.
.SH BUGS
A hunk whose header counts fewer lines than follow it leaves the rest as text between diffs,
and with
.B Pzero
a hunk added after a last line with no newline joins it to that line;
.IR git 's
apply does both the same.
